\begin{title}
Rational modulation of covalent organic frameworks heterogeneous catalyst: Structural cationization effect on accelerating photocatalytic oxidation process
\end{title}

Hengtao Lei 1, Changyan Zhu 1, Lin Lin, Xueyan Han, Yongyue Ding, Jian Song *, Yuyang Tian *, Guangshan Zhu *

Key Laboratory of Polyoxometalate and Reticular Material Chemistry of Ministry of Education, Faculty of Chemistry, Northeast Normal University, Changchun 130024, China

\begin{title}
A R T I C L E I N F O
\end{title}

Keywords: Covalent organic frameworks Heterogeneous photocatalyst Cationization effect Chemical warfare agents Selective oxidation Air atmosphere

\begin{title}
1. Introduction
\end{title}

As a promising avenue to utilize solar energy for production of valuable chemicals [1–3], water treatment [4,5], and even decontami­ nation of chemical warfare agent [6,7], visible light photocatalysis shows great potential in alleviating energy crises and environmental remediation. Heterogeneous photocatalysts with high efficiency and significant reusability have attracted widespread attention and recog­ nition due to their better implementation in industrial production [8,9]. Up to now, although various photocatalysts have been explored and developed, most materials still suffer from a quite poor photocatalytic activity due to the lack of absorption in visible light region, low charge separation and transport efficiency, or weak mass transfer ability. Therefore, it is crucial to design and regulate highly active heteroge­ neous photocatalysts under visible light. As we all know, the photo­ catalytic process involves a series of key procedures, such as optical absorption, separation and migration of photogenerated electron-hole pair, mass transfer and oxidation-reduction process [10–12]. The effi­ ciency of each step will directly affect the speed of the photocatalytic

