\begin{title}
ABＳTRＡCT
\end{title}

Photocatalytic technology has been playing a key role in energy conversion and environmental remediation. It is critical and challenging that understanding and modulating the local structure effect on accelerating each rate- dependent step during the photocatalytic process. Herein, we utilize the highly ordered and structurally tunable COFs as a favorable platform for integrating multiple modulations of photochemical properties, including light capture, exciton dissociation, charge separation, and mass transfer by structural cationization strategy. More­ over, it is found that the cationized COF has a strong enrichment ability on the sulfide substrates, exhibiting an extremely fast selective oxidation rate of sulfides even in air. The calculation results indicate that the structural cationization successfully aggregates reaction substrates and increases the molecular collision probability, generating a reaction environment to promote the photocatalytic performances. This study will be expected to facilitate the new insights for the exploration of highly active COFs heterogeneous photocatalysts.

process. In this context, it would be highly decisive to acquire in-depth insights into intrinsic underlying effects caused by changes in the local structure or chemical microenvironment of catalysts for modulating and accelerating each rate-dependent step during the entire photocatalytic process.

Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are a class of crystalline porous organic polymers composed of organic components periodically linked by covalent bonds [13–19], which have been applied in many fields such as adsorption [20,21], separation [22], catalysis [23], sensing [24], energy storage and conversion [25], etc. By decorating either the organic moieties or the covalent linkages, COFs have structural tunability and functional predictability. This allows COFs to easily adjust their optical and electronic properties from a structural perspective [26–29]. In addition, the high surface area and rich porosity of COFs allow high catalytic site exposure and rapid mass transfer, opening new opportunities for the design and development of effective photocatalysts. At present, several main strategies have been reported to enhance the catalytic activity of COFs heterogeneous photocatalysts, including the design of electron donor-acceptor architecture, forming

heterojunction composites, incorporating photosensitizer [11,30,31]. Moreover, regulating the catalytic reaction environment where the active sites are located is also very critical and directly linked to the conversion efficiency [32], such as solvation environment [33], unsat­ urated environment [34], superhydrophobic surface microenvironment [35], coordination environments [36,37]. Thus, it is very challenging that designing and modulating the structure of catalyst to accelerate multiple rate-dependent steps of photocatalytic system simultaneously, as additional consideration must be given to the process of catalyst absorbing light to produce and transfer active substances, and a subtle photocatalytic reaction environment.

In this work, we attempt to integrate multiple functionalities asso­ ciated with the cationic efficacy, including facilitating charge separation and enriching substrates [38,39], and utilize their synergistic effects to construct an efficient catalytic reaction environment in COFs, thereby accelerating the whole photocatalytic process. We first selected the porphyrin group as the photoactive center to react with the phenan­ throline linker to obtain a two-dimensional mesoporous COF, named Por-Phen-COF. By one-step cationic post-modification of the phenan­ throline linker, Por-Phen-COF-N+ was obtained (Fig. 1a). Subsequently, the photocatalytic oxidation of mustard gas simulant (chloroethyl ethyl sulfide, CEES) was chosen as the representative model reaction. Because the detoxification of mustard gas is extremely important to protect human health [40–42], and selective conversion of mustard gas to its sulfoxide product by photocatalytic oxidation is the most attractive due

