to its efficiency, sustainability and ease of operation. Meanwhile, it has been previously confirmed that there is a strong binding ability between cationic groups and organic sulfides [43,44], which is beneficial for enriching sulfide substrates and promoting the formation of catalytic reaction environments. Results show that simple cationization can effectively improve the light capture ability of COF, and the band gap narrows from 1.70 eV to 1.44 eV. Electron distribution of the excited state COFs has been also adjusted to achieve efficient and stable charge separation and transfer. Furthermore, Por-Phen-COF-N+ as a metal-free photocatalyst, shows an extremely fast detoxification rate for the CEES, and the half-life is only 3.4 min under air atmosphere, which is nearly 10 times shorter than that of as-synthesized Por-Phen-COF. Adsorption experiments and calculated adsorption energy results exhibit the excellent adsorption ability of cationized COF for CEES. Density func­ tional theory (DFT) calculations have further elucidated the mechanism of the efficient photocatalytic reaction. Compared with Por-Phen-COF, the significant changes in HOMO and LUMO of Por-Phen-COF-N+ fully demonstrate that the generated cationic reaction environment greatly promotes the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and the collision probability with reactive CEES intermediates, thereby accel­ erating the reaction process. This study emphasizes that simple catio­ nization can effectively regulate the photochemical properties of COFs from multiple aspects of light capture, exciton dissociation, charge transfer and mass transfer during the entire photocatalytic process. Along with the successful modulation and acceleration of multiple

rate-dependent steps, this will further help to understand the rational development of COFs heterogeneous photocatalysts in the future.

\begin{title}
2. Experimental section
\end{title}

\begin{title}
2.1. Synthesis of Por-Phen-COF
\end{title}

A Pyrex tube was charged with 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-aminophenyl) porphyrin (TAPP) (54 mg, 0.08 mmol), 4,4′-(1,10-phenanthroline-3,8- diyl)dibenzaldehyde (PDDA) (62 mg, 0.16 mmol), 1,2-Dichlorobenzene (1 mL), 1-Butanol (3 mL), and 6 M aqueous acetic acid (0.4 mL). The tube was degassed by freeze-pump-thaw technique for three times and then sealed under vacuum. The reaction was heated at 120 ◦C for 3 days yielding solid at the bottom of the tube. The resulting brown precipitate was filtered off and washed with dichloromethane, tetrahydrofuran, and methanol, respectively, then Soxhlet extracted with THF for 48 h. The product was dried at 80◦C under vacuum for overnight to obtain Por- Phen-COF (93 mg, Yield: 83 %).

\begin{title}
2.2. Synthesis of Por-Phen-COF-N+
\end{title}

A three-neck flask equipped with a magnetic stirring bar, dropping funnel and a reflux condenser was charged with Por-Phen-COF (300 mg, 0.44 mmol (molar number of phenanthroline), 1.0 equiv) and dissolved in nitrobenzene (25 mL). Afterwards, 1,3-dibromopropane (0.45 mL, 4.4 mmol, 10 equiv) dissolving in nitrobenzene (4 mL) was dropwise added and the mixture was stirred at 130 ◦C for 12 h. Then, the product was cooled to room temperature and collected by filtration, washed with n-hexane and methanol respectively and vacuum dried overnight to obtain Por-Phen-COF as a dark brown solid (452 mg, 93 % yields).

\begin{title}
2.3. Photocatalytic CEES degradation experiment using COFs
\end{title}

The Xe lamp was adjusted to a light intensity of 0.5 W⋅cm−2. COFs (1 %, 1.29 μmoL porphyrin unit) were dispersed in 3 mL of CD3OD/ CH3OH and placed in a quartz reactor and the mixture was sonicated for 2 min. The reactor was purged with pure oxygen or room air for 10 min, 15 μL (129 μmol) of CEES was added to the quartz reactor with a micro syringe. Then, the reactor was exposed to simulated solar light and monitored by NMR measurement.

\begin{title}
2.4. Reusability test
\end{title}

After the photocatalytic reaction, the catalyst was separated by filtration and washed thrice with CH3OH (10 mL). The powder was dried under vacuum. Then, the powder was added to CD3OD in a quartz reactor. After bringing in the required gas and activation under simu­ lated solar light for 10 min, fresh CEES (15 μL) was added to the mixture, and the resultant mixture was directly used for the next cata­ lytic cycle.

\begin{title}
2.5. Aerobic oxidation of sulfide to sulfoxide using COFs
\end{title}

The Xe lamp was adjusted to a light intensity of 0.5 W⋅cm−2. COFs (0.5 %, 1.5 μmoL porphyrin unit), organic sulfide (0.3 mmol) and in­ ternal standard (biphenyl, 0.3 mmol) were dispersed in 2 mL solvent (CD3OD/CH3OH) in a quartz reactor and the mixture was sonicated for 2 min. The reactor was purged with room air (0.1 MP) in the dark and stirred for 10 min. Then, the reactor was exposed to simulated solar light and monitored by GC-MS measurement.

\begin{title}
3. Results and discussion
\end{title}

\begin{title}
3.1. Synthesis and structure characterizations
\end{title}

The Por-Phen-COF was prepared via Schiff base condensation

reactions of 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-aminophenyl)porphyrin (TAPP) and 4,4′-(1,10-phenanthroline-3,8-diyl)dibenzaldehyde (PDDA) under sol­ vothermal conditions. Subsequently, 1,3-dibromopropane quaternized two N atoms on phenanthroline to obtain cationized Por-Phen-COF-N+. To ensure the success of post-modification, we successfully performed the model reaction using 1,10-Phenanthroline as a model compound (Fig. S1). All details of reaction procedures were listed in Supporting Information (Fig. S2) [45,46]. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analyses and theoretical structural simulation were performed to determine the crystalline structure of the as-synthesized Por-Phen-COF. As shown in Fig. 1b, the experimental PXRD patterns of Por-Phen-COF reveals high crystallinity with a set of sharp peaks at 2θ = 2.44◦, 4.83◦and 7.19◦, which corresponding to (100), (200), (300) planes, respectively. After structural optimization by Pawley refinements, the experimental data well matches the simulated PXRD patterns of the AA-stacking model (a = 38.49 Å, b = 37.64 Å, c = 3.40 Å, α = β = γ = 90◦, Rp = 5.90 % and Rwp = 7.36 %) (Table S1). In the high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM) images, the oriented lattice fringes with spacing of 3.25 nm have been clearly observed, which basically matches the simulated AA-stacking channels of Por-Phen-COF along the [001] di­ rection (Fig. 1c). Significantly, both PXRD patterns (Fig. 2a) of post-modified Por-Phen-COF-N+ fully demonstrate that the high crys­ tallinity of COF is well maintained after cationization.

Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy and solid-state 13C cross-polarization magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (CP/MAS NMR) spectroscopy were performed to study the bond for­ mation and cationization of Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+. In Fig. S3, the primary amine moiety of TAPP monomer at 3340 cm−1 and the aldehyde group of PDDA monomer at 1700 cm−1 are absent, while the new absorption peak at 1620 cm−1 corresponding to the character­ istic vibration of C––N bond is observed, confirming the successful for­ mation of imine linkage in COFs. For Por-Phen-COF-N+, the C––N linkage at 1620 cm−1 is retained, and a new stretching vibration band of C-H at 2950 cm−1 appears, which is ascribed to the -CH2 alkyl groups (Fig. S4). The solid-state 13C NMR further verified the successful modi­ fication of cationization. Specifically, Por-Phen-COF-N+ shows new signals at 30 ppm and 62 ppm (Fig. 2b and 2c), which are attributed to the sp2 carbon in the cation part. Furthermore, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to analyze the surface chemical environ­ ment of Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+. The high-resolution N 1 s spectrum of Por-Phen-COF was deconvoluted into pyrrole N (397.8 eV) in the porphyrin ring, imine bond N (399.0 eV) and pyridine N (399.8 eV) in phenanthroline (Fig. 2d). In contrast, the pyridine N peak in Por-Phen-COF-N+ is disappeared and a strong peak appears at 401.8 eV, which was attributed to the N+ cation obtained by cationi­ zation (Fig. 2e) [47,48]. In addition, High-resolution Br 3d spectra of Por-Phen-COF-N+ also displays two new peaks at 67.2 eV and 68.2 eV, which are attributed to Br 3d5/2 and Br 3d3/2, respectively, indicating the presence of bromide anions (Fig. 2f) [39,49]. Meanwhile, the TEM images and the corresponding elemental maps clearly show the uniform distribution of C, N and Br in Por-Phen-COF-N+ (Fig. 2g), revealing the successful introduction of cationization.

The porous structure of COFs has a significant impact on the mass transfer and accessibility of active sites in the photocatalytic system. Thus, the N2 adsorption-desorption isotherms was carried out under 77 K to investigate the porosity of COFs. As shown in Fig. 2h, the Por- Phen-COF exhibited typical type-IV isotherm, revealing the meso­ porous characteristics. After local cationization, Por-Phen-COF-N+ ex­ hibits a rapid rise similar to the type I curve in the low-pressure region, indicating that it has a strong interaction with N2. After the relative pressure is higher than 0.1, the curve still shows an upward trend rather than a platform, indicating that it contains a large amount of mesopores. In addition, near-reversible isotherms and small hysteresis loops are typical features of highly ordered materials. According to the Brunauer- Emmett-Teller (BET) model, the BET surface area of Por-Phen-COF is calculated to be 819 m2 g−1. The pore size distribution derived from the

