nonlocal density functional theory (NLDFT) method is centered at 3.40 nm (Fig. 2i), which is well in agreement with theoretical pore size of the AA-stacking model and the observed lattice fringes of HR-TEM images. After the cationization, the SBET changes to 227 m2 g−1 for Por-Phen-COF-N+ with the pore size of 2.70 nm. The decrease of BET surface area and pore narrowing of Por-Phen-COF-N+ are mainly attributed to the introduction of alkyl chains and Br– counterions into the pore walls to occupy the pores and the disorder of the local structure caused by ion repulsion after cationization [50,51]. The enough large pore size of cationic Por-Phen-COF-N+ may lead to more active site exposure and mass transfer, promoting the photocatalytic reaction.

Scanning electron microscope (SEM) measurements were employed to observe the two COFs microscopic crystalline morphology. The SEM images revealed that Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ keep similar nanorod morphology (Fig. S5). Furthermore, the TEM images demon­ strate that the two COFs have a long-range ordered high crystalline structure (Fig. S6). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curves indicate that COFs could maintain thermal stability up to 400 ◦C without sig­ nificant weight loss (Fig. S7). The chemical stability was evaluated by stirring the two COFs in different solvents for 3 days (Fig. S8). From the

