of Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ were determined by valence band X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (VB-XPS) (Fig. 3b) [54,55]. The valence band (VB) positions are 1.30 eV and 0.62 eV, respectively, and the conduction band (CB) is calculated to be −0.40 eV and −0.82 eV (Fig. 3c). To further demonstrate the rationality of the narrower band gap, the electronic structures of Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ are studied by density-functional theory (DFT) calculations. As shown in the density of states (DOS) distributions (Fig. 3d), compared with Por-Phen-COF, the CBM of Por-Phen-COF-N+ has a new peak near the Fermi level, resulting in a narrower theoretical band gap, which is consistent with the experimental results. Further analysis shows that the new peak is indeed derived from the introduced Br– and N+ (Fig. S11). Meanwhile, the introduction of Br– in Por-Phen-COF-N+ substantially enhance the DOS of the valence band maximum (VBM) in comparison to Por-Phen-COF. Therefore, in Por-Phen-COF-N+, more electrons can be photoexcited to the conduction band to participate in the photocatalytic reaction.

