electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and time-resolved pho­ toluminescence (TRPL) spectroscopy were performed. Transient photocurrent response results (Fig. 3e) disclose that the photocurrent intensity of Por-Phen-COF-N+ is enhanced by an order of magnitude compared with Por-Phen-COF. It indicates that Por-Phen-COF-N+ has higher photoinduced charge carrier mobility [56,57]. The electro­ chemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) (Fig. 3f) shows that the arc radius of Por-Phen-COF-N+ was much smaller than that of Por-Phen-COF under both dark and light conditions, indicating that it exhibits lower charge transfer resistance and faster interface charge transfer [58,59]. As shown in Fig. 3g, a low PL intensity was observed in Por-Phen-COF-N+ compared to that of Por-Phen-COF, indicating the effective separation of electron-hole pairs [60,61]. Time-resolved pho­ toluminescence spectroscopy (Fig. 3h) was also used to further investi­ gate the electron-hole pair separation kinetics of COFs. The results show that Por-Phen-COF-N+ possesses a longer average fluorescence lifetime of 6.98 ns compared to that of 2.91 ns for Por-Phen-COF, which is nearly 2.4 times higher. This indicates that Por-Phen-COF-N+ has higher pho­ togenerated carrier utilization ability [62–64]. Furthermore, to

