elucidate the photo-induced electron-hole pair recombination and sep­ aration kinetics, the temperature-dependent photoluminescence (PL) spectra were recorded (Fig. S12). Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ exhibit thermal quenching of PL emission in the temperature range of 83 K to 293 K, and the PL intensity at different temperatures are fitted by Arrhenius equation (Fig. 3i and S13) [65–67]. The exciton binding energies (Eb) of Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ are estimated to be 44 mV and 28 mV, respectively, demonstrating that excitons dissocia­ tion in Por-Phen-COF-N+ were more feasible to occurred due to the local cationization [68–70]. The above results indicate that the cationization significantly inhibits the recombination of electron-hole pairs, extends the charge carriers lifetime and promotes exciton separation. Mean­ while, the cationized COF has higher charge carrier mobility and faster transfer speed, which will lead to better photocatalytic activity of Por-Phen-COF-N+.

\begin{title}
3.3. Photocatalytic degradation of CEES and other sulfides performances
\end{title}

Mustard gas, as a typical class of chemical warfare agents, has caused seriously threat to social security in past chemical leakage accidents. Therefore, effective detoxification of mustard gas is essential. For safety reasons, CEES with similar molecular geometry and chemical properties to mustard gas is often used as a simulant for detoxification research. Based on the interaction of cations with negative charge in sulfides, the adsorption capacities of Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ for the CEES was initially studied. Acetonitrile was selected as the solvent, and the difference in adsorption capacity of Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen- COF-N+ for CEES was observed by stirring adsorption. The results show that although the specific surface area of Por-Phen-COF-N+ is smaller

