than that of Por-Phen-COF, its maximum adsorption capacity for CEES is 4.2 times that of Por-Phen-COF (Figs. S14 and S15), which proves that Por-Phen-COF-N+ containing cations in the skeleton has better inter­ action with CEES. DFT calculations were also performed to study the interaction between COFs and CEES molecules. The calculations results showed that Por-Phen-COF-N+ had a stronger interaction with CEES, because the adsorption energy was more closed to 0 eV (0.13 eV for Por- Phen-COF-N+ and 0.48 eV for Por-Phen-COF) (Fig. S16 and S17). Moreover, the CEES molecules are mainly adsorbed around the phe­ nanthroline of Por-Phen-COF and the cationic sections of Por-Phen-COF- N+. These results confirm that the electron-deficient structure in the skeleton plays a major role in the adsorption of CEES, and cationization promotes this phenomenon. In addition, the adsorption energies of the two COFs for O2 were also calculated (Fig. S18 and S19). The O2 adsorption energies of Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ were 0.80 eV and 0.07 eV, respectively, indicating that Por-Phen-COF-N+ exhibits a stronger adsorption effect on O2, enhancing its photocatalytic oxidation ability. Therefore, local cationization will be more conducive to the mass transfer of the substrate and O2 and the contact with the active center in the catalytic process.

The excellent optical properties and strong mass transfer ability endow it with the potential to detoxicate sulfur mustards. Thus, Por- Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ were used for the photocatalytic oxidation of CEES, to access their performances and compare with other catalysts. The detailed catalytic experimental process has been described in the supporting information. The results show that the oxidation of CEES into CEESO proceeds with 100 % selectivity, and no toxic sulfone derivative (2-chloroethyl ethyl sulfone, CEESO2) are detected (Fig. 4a). Under oxygen atmosphere, the kinetic curves are shown in the Fig. 4b.

