Por-Phen-COF as the catalyst, CEES is completely non-toxic within 67 min (Fig. S20), and the half-life (t1/2) is 19.2 min. In contrast, with the aid of Por-Phen-COF-N+, CEES can be completely converted within 6.5 min (Fig. S21), the t1/2 is evaluated to be 2.6 min. The faster cata­ lytic performance of Por-Phen-COF-N+ profit from the abundant cationic active sites with adsorption capacity and the greatly improved optical properties brought by cationization. To evaluate the practica­ bility of the COFs catalysts in the actual environment, we conducted degradation experiments under air atmosphere. The results show that Por-Phen-COF could achieve detoxification of CEES within 80.1 min (Fig. S22), and the t1/2 is 31.9 min (Fig. 4c). Surprisingly, Por-Phen- COF-N+ still shows an extraordinary conversion rate, achieving CEES detoxification within 6.5 min (Fig. S23), with a t1/2 of 3.4 min. To our knowledge, it is difficult to achieve the degradation of mustard gas under air conditions, and the catalytic performances of Por-Phen-COF- N+ is superior to most of porous material catalysts (Table S2) [6,71–74].

When the monomer TAPP was used as a control experiment, TAPP was dissolved in CD3OD, and the experiment was carried out in a ho­ mogeneous phase. The CEES conversion rate in 10 min was less than 90 %, and toxic by-products were generated (Fig. S24). Moreover, CEES detoxification was performed under the air atmosphere in CH3OH. The results showed that the detoxification effects of both COFs were weak­ ened to varying degrees (Fig. S25). CEES detoxification was also carried out under no light conditions, and the conversion rate did not exceed 5 % within 15 min (Fig. S26). To prove the reusability of high- performance catalysts, we carried out cyclic experiments on Por-Phen- COF-N+. The results show that after 6 cycles, the Por-Phen-COF-N+ still maintains the catalytic performances comparable to the original mate­ rial (Fig. 4d). The two COFs recovered after catalytic cycle were char­ acterized by FT-IR, 13C NMR, PXRD, XPS, SEM and TEM (Figs. S27-S28). The FT-IR still retained the-C––N bond of 1619 cm−1, and the-C-H bond

