The efficiency in CEES detoxification by Por-Phen-COF-N+ prompts investigations into its potential in the aerobic oxidation of sulfide under air [75,76]. Under the irradiation of Xe lamp, Por-Phen-COF and Por-Phen-COF-N+ were used for catalytic experiments (Figs. S30-S33). Por-Phen-COF-N+ showed excellent catalytic performance, which can complete more than 96 % conversion of most substrates within 60 min with good selectivity (Tables S4). In contrast, the conversion of sulfide by Por-Phen-COF is inferior (Tables S5). Considering that deuterated reagents can prolong the lifetime of singlet oxygen to promote the re­ action [77,78]. Deuterated methanol was chosen to repeat the experi­ ment. Surprisingly, the results show that with the participation of deuterated methanol, the conversion rates of sulfides by the two COFs have been improved to varying degrees (Table 1 and S6). Among them, Por-Phen-COF-N+ has a conversion rate of > 98 % for all selected sub­ strates within 45 min, and the selectivity is excellent, which proved that local cationization significantly improves the photocatalytic ability of COFs.

\begin{title}
3.4. Photocatalytic mechanism study
\end{title}

The molecular oxygen activation behavior of Por-Phen-COF and Por- Phen-COF-N+ were studied to in-depth comprehend the photoactivity mechanism. To investigate the ROS involved in this process, radical quenching experiments and electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy were carried out [79,80], which was convincing evidence

for the identification of ROS can be given. The 3,3′,5,5′-tetrame­ thylbenzidine (TMB) as a probe molecule. AgNO3, EDTA-2Na, p-ben­ zoquinone and β-carotene as quenchers to quench the e-, h+, O2•−and 1O2, respectively. As shown in Fig. 5a, Por-Phen-COF-N+ exhibits stronger oxidation ability than Por-Phen-COF without quenching agent. With the p-benzoquinone as O2•− scavenger and β-carotene as 1O2 scavenger addition (Fig. 5b), The oxidation of TMB by Por-Phen-COF was inhibited to varying degrees, indicating that both O2•− and 1O2 were involved in the reaction. It is worth noting that the oxidation of TMB by Por-Phen-COF-N+ is mainly dominated by 1O2, which is pointed out by the drastic quenching of the reaction caused by the addition of β-carotene as 1O2 scavenger. The addition of p-benzoquinone and EDTA-2Na led to a slight reaction quenching, which proved that O2•− and h+ were also involved in the oxidation reaction of Por-Phen-COF-N+. In addition, the results of quenching reaction showed that the ability of Por-Phen-COF-N+ to produce ROS was promoted after local cationization, and the ability of 1O2 generation was significantly improved, which may benefit from more effective charge separation and transport. Next, 5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline-oxide (DMPO) and 2,2,6, 6-tetramethylpiperidine (TEMP) were added to the photocatalytic re­ action as spin trapping agents, respectively (Fig. 5c and S34). The signals belonging to O2 •−and 1O2 were observed under light conditions, which further proved that O2 •−and 1O2 both initiated by two COFs for catalytic oxidation.

