significant performance enhancement effects of constructed COFs het­ erogeneous photocatalyst by cationization have been proposed and proved. Under light irradiation, the exposed cationic sites make COFs have extensive and strong light capture ability, continuous and rapid photogenerated charge carrier separation/transfer abilities, and pro­ vides active sites for the adsorption of the substrates and O2, which endow it with fast mass transfer ability to reaction substrates in the photocatalytic process. Compared with the original Por-Phen-COF, the cationized Por-Phen-COF-N+ has significantly improved photocatalytic activity. Under oxygen atmosphere, the half-life complete degradation of CEES with Por-Phen-COF-N+ is only 2.6 min. Under air atmosphere, the half-life of CEES degradation also reaches a surprising 3.4 min, which is an order of magnitude higher than that of the original material. In addition, the cationized Por-Phen-COF-N+ also exhibits faster con­ version rate and better selectivity for aerobic oxidation of sulfides. The photocatalytic mechanism study based on theoretical calculations further proves the key role of structural cationic efficacy in improving photocatalytic performances. This work provides new insight for enhancing the photocatalytic performance of COFs from multiple per­ spectives, which is conducive to promoting the design of porous photocatalysts.

